‘Glaube und Schönheit’ from 17

inhalt.text:
There were state boarding schools called Nationalpolitische Erziehungsanstalten, intended to train a future leadership elite. {{1}}
The main characters Friedrich and Albrecht really lived at a Napola. {{2}}
Applicants were selected according to ‘racial’ and physical criteria; tests of courage were part of the process. {{3}}
Boxing, drill and field exercises were part of everyday life at the elite schools. {{4}}
Napola pupils wore no uniform and could freely choose their subjects. {{5}}
Boys from modest backgrounds could also be admitted – school fees depended on their parents' income. {{6}}
By 1945, there were around 40 such institutions. {{7}}
The Napolas were run by the Hitlerjugend, not the state. {{8}}
Many former pupils joined the Wehrmacht as officer candidates, and many were killed in the war. {{9}}
